  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 0,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Depending on what you actually count as a \nvideo game, the industry is now generating  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4400,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "somewhere between 200 and $350 billion a year \nin annual revenue, making it by far the largest  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11120,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "entertainment sector in the world. Regardless \nof whatever your exact definitions may be, more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15880,
    "dDurationMs": 4909,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "people are playing video games than ever before. \nThey are playing them for longer. And the notso  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20789,
    "dDurationMs": 4811,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "secret reality is that video game companies have \ngotten a lot better at getting money out of them  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25600,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "while they are. The vast vast majority of revenue \nin this industry now comes from in-game purchases  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 31640,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in advertising, even amongst games that already \nhave an upfront purchase cost or a recurring  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36760,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "subscription. Now, obviously, this hasn't been \ngreat for gamers. But at least a growing user  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 41520,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "base of people playing longer, spending more, and \ngetting advertised to all across more platforms  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46480,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has been good for the shareholders, right? Wrong. \nSome of the largest companies in the industry have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52080,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "experienced huge stock declines, multiple rounds \nof restructuring, narrowly avoided bankruptcy,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 57000,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass layoffs, and divevestatures. Over what should \nhave been the best half decade in the history of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 62160,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the industry, the overall market is down by as \nmuch as 30% from its peak. In the same time that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 67480,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the broader market has more than doubled. So, \nhow is it possible that even with record sales,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 72640,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "record new revenue streams, record numbers of \nnew players, and record new opportunities to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 76800,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "monetize that new audience, video game companies \nhave still managed to screw this up so badly.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82320,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Video game maker Electronic Arts announcing a \n$55 billion deal to go private and what is set  "
    } ]
  }, {
